<h1>2D fog of war</h1>
<p><code>demi.visibility.fog2d</code> draws a smooth world-space fog mask after 2D world sprites and particles, before the HUD. The CPU supplies a visibility polygon and discovery cells; a native GPU shader performs edge coverage, range falloff, memory interpolation and compositing. No per-frame mask bitmap is rasterized on the CPU. It is suitable for top-down exploration; it is not a lighting or stealth simulation.</p>
<pre><code>local Fog = require("demi.visibility.fog2d")
assert(Fog.configure(60, 46, 1, -0.5, -0.5, 0.025, 0.028, 0.05, 0.45))
Fog.reveal(5, 5)
assert(Fog.update(player.x, player.y, 11, 1.4, "wall"))</code></pre>
<p><code>configure(width, height, cell_size, origin_x, origin_y, r, g, b, memory_opacity)</code> replaces the mask. Dimensions are integer cell counts from 1 to 256, the cell size is positive, and origin is the lower-left map corner. Colors and memory opacity use normalized values. Invalid parameters return <code>false</code> without replacing existing state. New cells start unexplored.</p>
<p><code>reveal(x, y)</code> remembers a zero-based cell; out-of-bounds cells are ignored. Discovery changes publish on the next rendered frame; <code>update</code> refreshes sight geometry separately. Persist these discovery coordinates in the game's save format and reveal them again after loading a scene.</p>
<p><code>update(x, y, radius, feather, occluder_layer)</code> uses native 2D collider rays around the observer. Radius is in world units, greater than zero and at most 256; feather is a positive inward fade distance at the maximum sight radius. Wall hits clip at their collider surface, independently of this range fade; paired rays refine blocker corners and fractional polygon coverage provides a narrow antialiased edge. Disabled colliders do not block sight, so opening a disabled door updates both physics and the next sight mask. The shader computes fractional coverage independently of pixel-art sprite filtering. Call after observer or blocker changes; avoid rebuilding unchanged masks.</p>
<p>This API controls presentation only. Enemies, targeting, AI sensing, minimap markers, and discovery rules remain gameplay responsibilities. Do not use translucent remembered terrain as evidence that an enemy is currently visible. <code>clear()</code> removes the mask; replacing the World also releases its state. There is one mask per World, not a per-camera multiplayer vision system.</p>
